---
import Image from '../assets/og/social-paper.webp';
import Article from '../components/article/Article.astro';
import ArticleAside from '../components/article/ArticleAside.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../components/article/ArticleHeader.astro';
import ArticleListItem from '../components/article/ArticleListItem.astro';
import DesignPrinciple from '../components/article/DesignPrinciple.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/article/Diagram.astro';
import H2 from '../components/article/H2.astro';
import H3 from '../components/article/H3.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../components/article/TableOfContents.astro';
import DualLanes from '../components/diagrams/DualLanes.astro';
import FeedPipeline from '../components/diagrams/FeedPipeline.astro';
import IdentityFlow from '../components/diagrams/IdentityFlow.astro';
import LocalIntent from '../components/diagrams/LocalIntent.astro';
import SystemOverview from '../components/diagrams/SystemOverview.astro';
import U from '../components/U.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const image_url = new URL(Image.src, Astro.site);

const description =
  'The Technical Paper describes the architecture of the platform: EUDI Wallet identity under eIDAS 2.0, architecturally separated public and private lanes, user-chosen feeds with on-device intent, anonymized aggregate post statistics, and open-source code with config in the repository.';

const parts = [
  {
    id: 'part-i',
    part: 'Part I',
    title: 'Identity Verification Architecture',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-ii',
    part: 'Part II',
    title: 'Interaction Architecture: Public and Private Lanes',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-iii',
    part: 'Part III',
    title: 'Privacy Architecture: Local-Only by Design',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-iv',
    part: 'Part IV',
    title: 'Anonymized Aggregate Metrics',
  },
  {
    id: 'part-v',
    part: 'Part V',
    title: 'System Synthesis and Open Governance',
  },
];
---

<Layout size="large">
  <Fragment slot="header">
    <title>Technical Paper &mdash; The Civia Project</title>

    <meta name="description" content={description} />

    <!-- Open Graph -->
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:image" content={image_url} />

    <!-- X Card -->
    <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
    <meta name="twitter:image" content={image_url} />
  </Fragment>

  <Article>
    <ArticleHeader
      label="Technical Paper"
      title="Engineering a Verified, Privacy-First Social Network"
      authors={['Alexandru Ifrim', 'Ștefan Alexandru Mocanu']}
      date="2026-06-22"
    />

    <ArticleAside label="Abstract" italic>
      <p>
        This paper is the technical companion to <a
          href="/social-paper"
          class="text-white underline decoration-zinc-600 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-white"
          >The Social Paper</a
        >. Where that document establishes the case for rebuilding social media,
        this one describes the architecture we are building: trust boundaries,
        data flows, and standards-aligned design decisions.
      </p>
      <p>
        The platform will initially target the European Union market. Every
        account is tied to a verified natural person through the <U
          >EUDI Wallet</U
        > under the <U>eIDAS 2.0</U> framework.
      </p>
      <p>
        Interaction is split into two architecturally separated lanes: a <U
          >public</U
        > lane for conventional social posting, and a <U>private</U> lane where friend-only
        posts are encrypted via Signal's <U>Whisper Protocol</U>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users choose their feed type — reverse-chronological or algorithmic,
        based on on-device behaviour. When algorithmic, feed intent is computed
        locally on the user's device and used only to query items for the feed.
        The platform does not build persistent behavioral profiles or long-term
        interest graphs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Only coarse, anonymized engagement aggregates are collected to show post
        statistics.
      </p>
      <p>
        The codebase is <U>open source</U>. Production configuration lives in
        the repository; only keys and other secrets are supplied at runtime.
      </p>
    </ArticleAside>

    <TableOfContents items={parts} />

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-i" part="Part I">Identity Verification Architecture</H2>

      <H3 id="part-i-verified-identity-as-a-primitive"
        >Verified Identity as a Structural Primitive</H3
      >
      <p>
        The open Web has no native mechanism to distinguish a human from a bot.
        As described in <a
          href="/social-paper#part-iv"
          class="text-white underline decoration-zinc-600 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-white"
          >The Social Paper</a
        >, this absence has enabled bot networks, social media farms, and
        AI-driven synthetic accounts to dominate the digital town square. For a
        platform whose mission is genuine human discourse, identity verification
        is not a feature bolted on at the edge — it is a structural primitive of
        the architecture.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every user account must be linked to a verified digital identity. The
        platform enforces a simple hierarchy: one human, one account. Bots,
        automated agents, and unverified entities cannot hold user accounts.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-i-eudi-wallet-and-eidas">EUDI Wallet and eIDAS 2.0</H3>
      <p>
        Identity verification on the platform relies on the <U
          >European Digital Identity Wallet</U
        > (EUDI Wallet), compliant with <U>Regulation (EU) 2024/1183</U> (eIDAS 2.0).
        Users hold government-issued digital credentials locally in their wallet.
        At registration, the platform receives only what the user explicitly chooses
        to disclose — not full identity documents, and not more attributes than the
        platform requires to operate.
      </p>
      <p>
        This approach aligns identity assurance with data minimization: the
        wallet is the user's agent, and the platform is a relying party that
        requests the minimum proof necessary.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-i-selective-disclosure-and-zkp"
        >Selective Disclosure and Zero-Knowledge Proofs</H3
      >
      <p>
        Where technically feasible, the platform employs <U
          >selective disclosure</U
        > and <U>zero-knowledge proofs</U> so that users can prove specific claims
        without revealing underlying personal data. For example:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Proof of natural person:</U> confirm the account holder is a real human
          without transmitting a full identity document.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Proof of legal age:</U> confirm a user has attained eighteen (18) years
          of age without revealing their precise date of birth — required for age-gated
          features such as opt-in algorithmic feeds.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The platform does not store raw identity credentials or full personal
        identifiers beyond what is strictly necessary for verification purposes,
        consistent with Articles 5, 25, and 32 of the GDPR.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-i-pseudonym-separation">Pseudonym Separation</H3>
      <p>
        Identity verification is required at registration, but users engage on
        the platform under a chosen pseudonym or handle. The link between a
        user's verified identity and their on-platform activity is protected by
        strong technical and organizational measures:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Cryptographic separation between identity verification records and
          social activity data.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Access controls ensuring the mapping between a verified identity and a
          user account is accessible only under lawful process or explicit user
          request.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          The platform does not proactively link or publish a user's real
          identity alongside their platform activity, except where required by a
          valid legal obligation.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <Diagram
        caption="Identity verification flow: EUDI Wallet to pseudonym separation"
      >
        <IdentityFlow />
      </Diagram>

      <DesignPrinciple id="principle-i" title="People First, Bots Never">
        <p>
          Verified digital identity is the foundation of a trustworthy social
          network. By tying every account to a real, accountable person through
          EUDI Wallet credentials, selective disclosure, and pseudonym
          separation, the platform structurally excludes bots and unverified
          agents — not through moderation alone, but through architecture.
        </p>
      </DesignPrinciple>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-ii" part="Part II">
        Interaction Architecture: Public and Private Lanes
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-ii-two-modes-one-platform">Two Modes, One Platform</H3>
      <p>
        Every post on the platform is explicitly <U>public</U> or <U>private</U
        >. Users choose visibility at publish time. The two lanes share identity
        verification and account infrastructure, but their data paths are
        architecturally separated — a private post never enters the public
        vector store.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-ii-public-interaction">Public Interaction</H3>
      <p>
        The public lane works like conventional social media for distribution
        and discovery. Posts are stored on platform servers, indexed into a
        vector database, and discoverable via follows and public surfaces. The
        reverse-chronological feed is the default. Verified adults may
        explicitly opt in to an algorithmic feed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The algorithmic feed on both lanes follows a strict six-step pipeline.
        Intent is <em>not</em> sent for ranking — it is sent to <U
          >query the vector database</U
        >. Ranking happens <em>after</em> seen-post filtering.
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-zinc-300">
        <li>
          The user's <U>intent vector</U> is computed <strong>locally</strong> on
          the device from on-device signals.
        </li>
        <li>
          The intent vector is sent to the <strong>server</strong>, together
          with a compact probabilistic filter snapshot for seen-post
          deduplication.
        </li>
        <li>
          The server uses the intent vector to <strong
            >query the vector database</strong
          > where post vectors are stored, returning a <strong
            >list of post IDs</strong
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          The server passes that ID list through <strong
            >probabilistic data structures</strong
          > (Bloom, Cuckoo, and Quotient filters) to remove already-seen posts.
        </li>
        <li>
          The server runs the <strong>ranking model</strong> on the filtered ID list.
        </li>
        <li>
          The server <strong>returns the ranked feed list</strong> to the client.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <Diagram
        caption="Feed pipeline shared by both lanes: query, filter, then rank"
      >
        <FeedPipeline />
      </Diagram>

      <H3 id="part-ii-private-interaction">Private Interaction</H3>
      <p>
        When a user publishes privately, the post is encrypted on the client
        before it leaves the device. It is delivered to all friends using <U
          >Signal's Whisper Protocol</U
        > (Double Ratchet and X3DH key agreement). Friends decrypt on their own devices.
      </p>
      <p>
        The private feed uses the same intent-driven pipeline as the public
        lane. Intent is computed on the user's device and sent to query a <strong
          >separate private vector store</strong
        > — indexed from ciphertext metadata, never from plaintext. The server relays
        encrypted payloads and executes vector queries, seen-post filtering, and ranking
        per feed request. It holds no decryption keys and sees no private post content.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-ii-friend-graph-and-boundaries"
        >Friend Graph and Cross-Lane Boundaries</H3
      >
      <p>
        Private delivery requires an established friend relationship. Key
        exchange and session management follow Whisper Protocol semantics. The
        public and private data paths never merge: content that enters the
        private lane cannot enter the public vector store or surface through the
        public feed pipeline.
      </p>

      <Diagram caption="Public and private interaction lanes">
        <DualLanes />
      </Diagram>

      <DesignPrinciple
        id="principle-ii"
        title="Public Discovery, Private by Protocol"
      >
        <p>
          Users choose visibility at publish time. Both lanes use on-device
          intent to query their respective vector stores, with a shared
          filter-then-rank pipeline. Private posts are encrypted and delivered
          to friends via the Whisper Protocol; the server relays ciphertext
          only. Two modes, one platform — architecturally separated.
        </p>
      </DesignPrinciple>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-iii" part="Part III">
        Privacy Architecture: Local-Only by Design
      </H2>

      <H3 id="part-iii-server-roles">Server Roles by Lane</H3>
      <p>
        On the <U>public lane</U>, the server stores public posts, the follow
        graph, and content vectors. It executes vector database queries,
        seen-post filtering, and ranking per feed request. It does not build
        persistent user profiling from intent vectors or filter snapshots
        received during those requests.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <U>private lane</U>, the server is structurally incapable of
        reading private content. It relays ciphertext between friends, holds no
        decryption keys, and queries a separate private vector store per feed
        request — using the same ephemeral intent-and-filter flow as the public
        lane.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-intent-computation-and-transmission"
        >Intent Computation and Transmission</H3
      >
      <p>
        On both lanes, the device computes the intent vector locally from
        on-device signals — the user controls what forms the query. The vector
        is transmitted to the server <em>only</em> to query the vector database for
        that specific feed request (public or private). It is not a direct input to
        the ranking model, and it is not retained to build a longitudinal interest
        graph.
      </p>
      <p>
        This distinction matters: per-request intent transmission is necessary
        for vector-based retrieval. Persistent profiling is not.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-seen-post-filters"
        >Seen-Post Filters: Probabilistic Data Structures</H3
      >
      <p>
        To suppress already-viewed posts from the feed, the platform uses <U
          >probabilistic data structures</U
        > — specifically <U>Bloom filters</U>, <U>Cuckoo filters</U>, and <U
          >Quotient filters</U
        >. These structures are <strong>maintained on-device</strong>. A compact
        snapshot is sent with each feed request so the server can apply them to
        the post ID list in step four, <em>before</em> ranking.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Why probabilistic:</U> fixed-size structures trade a controlled false-positive
          rate for orders-of-magnitude less storage than exact sets. A false positive
          may occasionally hide a post the user has not seen; this is an acceptable
          trade-off for feed deduplication at scale.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Bloom filters</U> for simple membership checks.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Cuckoo filters</U> where deletion and eviction of stale entries matters.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Quotient filters</U> as a space-efficient alternative with configurable
          false-positive bounds.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Cross-device sync:</U> canonical filter state is synced across the user's
          devices via an end-to-end encrypted personal data mesh. The server receives
          only ephemeral snapshots per request.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <H3 id="part-iii-local-state-and-sync"
        >Local State and Cross-Device Synchronization</H3
      >
      <p>
        User preferences — blocks, filters, themes — and interaction history
        remain on-device across both lanes. Personal state is synchronized
        across the user's own devices via E2EE relays or direct peer-to-peer
        connections. The platform's servers act as blind couriers for sync
        traffic; they never hold decryption keys or the master copy of personal
        data.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-minor-accounts">Minor Accounts</H3>
      <p>
        Users verified as minors receive a chronological feed only on both
        lanes. Algorithmic recommendations are not available, regardless of of
        parental consent. Age is proven via selective disclosure or
        zero-knowledge proof without revealing the user's precise date of birth.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iii-what-we-do-not-collect"
        >What the Platform Does Not Collect</H3
      >
      <p>The platform does not collect or retain:</p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Persistent behavioral profiles or cross-session interest graphs.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          "Privacy toggle" mining — preferences used as psychological data
          points.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Audience profiling dossiers derived from platform activity.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>Private post plaintext.</ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Long-term server-side read-history logs.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Minimal server-side data exists for platform function: public posts, the
        follow graph, verification tokens, and encrypted private payloads as
        ciphertext.
      </p>

      <Diagram
        caption="Device-origin intent and filters versus ephemeral server-side processing"
      >
        <LocalIntent />
      </Diagram>

      <DesignPrinciple
        id="principle-iii"
        title="Privacy as Structure, Not Setting"
      >
        <p>
          Privacy is woven into the architecture — not offered as a toggle
          buried in settings. Intent is computed locally; filters are maintained
          on-device; the server processes feed requests ephemerally without
          building surveillance dossiers. The private lane is encrypted by
          protocol, not by policy.
        </p>
      </DesignPrinciple>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-iv" part="Part IV">Anonymized Aggregate Metrics</H2>

      <H3 id="part-iv-purpose">Purpose</H3>
      <p>
        The platform collects only coarse, anonymized metrics for operational
        health: DAU and MAU bands, post volume trends, error rates, and
        infrastructure capacity. These metrics exist to keep the platform
        running — not to profile individuals.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iv-what-is-collected">What Is Collected</H3>
      <p>Generalized counters only, for example:</p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>Public posts created today.</ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>Encrypted payloads relayed this week.</ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>Active sessions in a given period.</ArticleListItem>
      </ul>
      <p>
        No per-user behavioral dossiers are constructed from these aggregates.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iv-public-vs-private-metrics"
        >Public vs Private Lane Implications</H3
      >
      <p>
        On the public lane, metrics may include coarse content-volume counts. On
        the private lane, metrics are limited to relay volume and delivery
        success — never message content, and never per-recipient engagement
        graphs.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-iv-what-is-forbidden">What Is Forbidden</H3>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Per-user engagement graphs and interest inference from aggregate data.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Re-identification of individuals from aggregated statistics.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>Third-party analytics SDKs.</ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          Mining private ciphertext metadata beyond delivery health.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <H3 id="part-iv-anonymization-and-transparency"
        >Anonymization and Transparency</H3
      >
      <p>
        Aggregation thresholds prevent fine-grained segmentation that could
        deanonymize small groups. Users who explicitly opt in may contribute
        broader anonymized data to help improve platform models — but only with
        informed, affirmative consent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Aggregate metrics are reported publicly. The platform publishes an
        annual disclosure of its data practices.
      </p>

      <DesignPrinciple id="principle-iv" title="Metrics Without Surveillance">
        <p>
          Operational metrics are necessary; individual surveillance is not. The
          platform measures its own health through anonymized counters — never
          through per-user behavioral dossiers or interest graphs derived from
          aggregate data.
        </p>
      </DesignPrinciple>
    </section>

    <section>
      <H2 id="part-v" part="Part V">System Synthesis and Open Governance</H2>

      <H3 id="part-v-component-overview">Component Overview</H3>
      <p>
        The architecture ties together identity verification, dual interaction
        lanes, and anonymized metrics into a single coherent system. Every layer
        enforces the same principle: serve users, protect privacy, verify
        humanity.
      </p>

      <Diagram caption="Full platform architecture synthesis">
        <SystemOverview />
      </Diagram>

      <H3 id="part-v-open-source-and-auditability"
        >Open Source and Auditability</H3
      >
      <p>
        The platform's code, algorithms, and identity flows are open to public
        scrutiny. Independent security audits, a public bug bounty program, and
        transparent governance ensure the architecture described in this paper
        matches the system users actually run.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-v-compliance">Compliance Framing</H3>
      <p>
        The platform is designed in alignment with applicable EU law. The
        following frameworks inform its architecture — not as checkboxes, but as
        structural constraints on how the system is built and operated.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-v-compliance-gdpr">GDPR</H3>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Privacy by design and by default</U> (Article 25) — protective settings
          are the default; personal data processing is limited to what the architecture
          requires.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Data minimization</U> (Article 5) — only data adequate, relevant, and
          necessary for stated purposes is processed.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Data Protection Impact Assessments</U> (Article 35) — conducted for high-risk
          processing activities, including identity verification and feed personalization.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>

      <H3 id="part-v-compliance-eidas">eIDAS 2.0</H3>
      <p>
        Digital identity verification follows <U>Regulation (EU) 2024/1183</U>
        (eIDAS 2.0) and the EUDI Wallet framework: selective disclosure, zero-knowledge
        proofs where feasible, and no storage of raw credentials beyond what verification
        requires.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-v-compliance-dsa">Digital Services Act (DSA)</H3>
      <p>
        Serving EU users as a very large online platform, it is designed with
        <U>Regulation (EU) 2022/2065</U> (the Digital Services Act) in mind — particularly
        its obligations around the protection of minors and transparency of recommender
        systems.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Article 28 — Protection of minors:</U> the architecture enforces age
          verification through the identity layer (selective disclosure or zero-knowledge
          proof of legal age). Accounts verified as belonging to a minor receive a
          chronological feed only; algorithmic recommendations are permanently suppressed
          at the technical level, regardless of parental consent.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>No profiling of minors:</U> activity data from minor accounts is used
          solely to deliver core platform functionality and safety enforcement — not
          to infer interests, build profiles, or train recommendation models.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Recommender system transparency (Article 27):</U> for verified adult
          users who explicitly opt in to algorithmic feeds, the platform discloses
          what data drives recommendations, the purpose and logic of the system, and
          how to deactivate the feature. The feed pipeline described in Part II —
          vector query, seen-post filtering, ranking — is open to public audit.
        </ArticleListItem>
        <ArticleListItem>
          <U>Additional minor safeguards:</U> privacy settings default to the most
          protective configuration for minor accounts; direct messaging from non-connected
          users is restricted; and regular technical audits verify that no recommendation
          pathways are accessible to minor accounts through standard interfaces or
          APIs.
        </ArticleListItem>
      </ul>
      <p>
        DSA compliance is not a layer applied after the fact. Chronological
        defaults, absolute algorithmic suppression for minors, and verified
        identity are architectural decisions that satisfy these obligations by
        construction.
      </p>

      <H3 id="part-v-honest-limitations">Honest Limitations</H3>
      <p>
        This paper states principles; the build tests them. Identity frameworks
        outside the European Union will be integrated as they mature. The
        local-first model requires capable client devices. Age verification,
        sustainable non-profit funding, and cross-border identity
        interoperability remain active areas of work.
      </p>

      <DesignPrinciple id="principle-v" title="The Commitment to Build" final>
        <p>
          Identifying the necessary architecture is only the first step. We are
          building this platform in public — open source, transparent, and
          governed by a non-profit organization whose sole mission is to steward
          a social network engineered for human benefit.
        </p>
        <p>
          The principles in this paper are the blueprint. The code is the proof.
        </p>
      </DesignPrinciple>
    </section>
  </Article>
</Layout>
